# Batch Settings

In this notebook you tune two settings for a simulated Elixir workload: the
batch size and the number of workers. Tune tries every combination with grid
search, picks the configuration with the highest simulated throughput, and
then checks the winner against the original settings on fresh workloads. The
notebook runs offline and needs no credentials.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tune, "~> 0.5.0"}
])
```

## Model the workload

Each workload is a tuple `{ideal_batch_size, ideal_workers}`. The simulator
starts at 100 and subtracts a penalty for each unit of distance from the ideal
settings. A wrong worker count costs three times as much as a wrong batch size.

```elixir
throughput = fn config, {ideal_size, ideal_workers} ->
  100 - abs(config.batch_size - ideal_size) - 3 * abs(config.workers - ideal_workers)
end

throughput.(%{batch_size: 1, workers: 1}, {7, 2})
```

The current settings, a batch size of 1 and one worker, score 91 on a workload
whose ideal is `{7, 2}`.

## Declare the search space

`Tune.new/2` takes the current settings as the target and the simulator as the
runner. `Tune.vary/2` names the fields that search may change. Grid search
needs finite values: an integer range or a list of choices.

```elixir
study =
  Tune.new(%{batch_size: 1, workers: 1}, run: throughput)
  |> Tune.data(validation: [{7, 2}, {7, 2}], test: [{6, 2}, {8, 3}])
  |> Tune.vary(batch_size: 1..16, workers: [1, 2, 3])
  |> Tune.score(:throughput, & &1)
  |> Tune.search(:grid)
```

The validation split is the data that search scores. The test split stays
unseen until the final comparison.

Grid search sorts the varied fields by name and walks them in nested order.
Here `batch_size` is the outer loop and `workers` is the inner loop, so the
grid holds 16 × 3 = 48 configurations:

```elixir
for batch_size <- 1..16, workers <- [1, 2, 3] do
  %{batch_size: batch_size, workers: workers}
end
|> Enum.take(4)
```

```text
[
  %{workers: 1, batch_size: 1},
  %{workers: 2, batch_size: 1},
  %{workers: 3, batch_size: 1},
  %{workers: 1, batch_size: 2}
]
```

## Search the full grid

`Tune.tune/1` scores every configuration on the same validation workloads and
returns a `Tune.Result`.

```elixir
{:ok, winner} = Tune.tune(study)

IO.puts("Evaluated #{length(winner.history)} configurations")
IO.puts("Selected #{inspect(winner.value)}")
IO.puts("Mean throughput #{winner.best.evaluation.summaries.throughput.mean}")
IO.puts("Stop reason #{winner.stop_reason}, complete? #{winner.complete?}")
```

```text
Evaluated 48 configurations
Selected %{workers: 2, batch_size: 7}
Mean throughput 100.0
Stop reason completed, complete? true
```

`winner.history` keeps each configuration in the order grid search tried it.
`winner.best` is the candidate with the highest mean score.

## Limit the number of evaluations

`Tune.limit/2` caps the work. With `evaluations: 4`, grid search stops after
the first four configurations and returns the best one it has seen.

```elixir
{:ok, partial} = study |> Tune.limit(evaluations: 4) |> Tune.tune()

IO.puts("Tried #{inspect(Enum.map(partial.history, & &1.value))}")
IO.puts("Selected #{inspect(partial.value)}")
IO.puts("Stop reason #{partial.stop_reason}, complete? #{partial.complete?}")
```

```text
Tried [%{workers: 1, batch_size: 1}, %{workers: 2, batch_size: 1}, %{workers: 3, batch_size: 1}, %{workers: 1, batch_size: 2}]
Selected %{workers: 2, batch_size: 1}
Stop reason evaluation_limit, complete? false
```

The limited run never reaches a batch size of 7. Its result marks the search
as incomplete, so a caller can tell a capped search from a finished one.

## Compare on fresh workloads

`Tune.compare/3` runs the original target and the winner on the same split and
pairs their scores case by case. Use the test split, which search never saw.

```elixir
{:ok, comparison} = Tune.compare(study, winner, split: :test)

for id <- comparison.case_ids do
  change = comparison.rows[id].metrics.throughput
  IO.puts("Case #{id}: #{change.baseline} -> #{change.challenger} (#{change.status}, +#{change.delta})")
end

metric = comparison.metrics.throughput
IO.puts("Mean change #{metric.delta} over #{metric.paired_count} paired cases")
```

```text
Case 0: 92 -> 99 (improved, +7)
Case 1: 87 -> 96 (improved, +9)
Mean change 8.0 over 2 paired cases
```

The winner improves both test workloads, even though neither matches the
validation ideal exactly.

These numbers come from the simulator, not from a running system. A higher
simulated score says the winner fits this model of the workload. Before you
change production settings, measure real throughput with the winning
configuration under representative load.

